WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.992
You need FX quotes from three providers.

2
00:00:05.992 --> 00:00:12.266
Northbank answers in 300 milliseconds, fjord-fx in 120, and tidewater in 200.

3
00:00:12.266 --> 00:00:17.644
Ask them one after another, and the caller waits 620 milliseconds.

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.118
But the calls don't need each other.

5
00:00:20.118 --> 00:00:26.816
Give each one its own lane, start them all at once, and you only wait for the slowest: 300 milliseconds.

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.178
In Kotlin, a builder starts each lane.

7
00:00:30.178 --> 00:00:34.250
A builder is a plain function that starts a child coroutine.

8
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.878
The first one is launch.

9
00:00:35.878 --> 00:00:40.221
It returns at once, with a Job: a handle you can join or cancel.

10
00:00:40.221 --> 00:00:41.713
The caller carries on.

11
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.486
But a Job holds no value.

12
00:00:43.486 --> 00:00:48.472
So launch is for work whose result you don't need, like writing an audit row.

13
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.913
For the quotes, use async.

14
00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:52.625
It's launch with a result.

15
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:58.159
It also returns at once, with a Deferred: a ticket for a value that isn't there yet.

16
00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.459
Now call awaitAll.

17
00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:03.148
The caller suspends, and its thread is free for other work.

18
00:01:03.148 --> 00:01:05.649
Each ticket fills when its call answers.

19
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.497
Done at 300 milliseconds, the slowest call.

20
00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:14.581
All three can even share one thread, because none of them uses it while it waits for the network.

21
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:16.561
Now a small change.

22
00:01:16.561 --> 00:01:19.355
Await each call right after you start it.

23
00:01:19.355 --> 00:01:20.855
How long does it take?

24
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:24.381
620 again.

25
00:01:24.381 --> 00:01:27.595
Each await ends before the next async starts.

26
00:01:27.595 --> 00:01:32.238
It compiles, it passes the tests, and it runs one call at a time.

27
00:01:32.238 --> 00:01:36.024
The overlap lives in the gap between async and await.

28
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:38.481
Every child needs a parent.

29
00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.394
In a suspend function, that's coroutineScope.

30
00:01:41.394 --> 00:01:46.055
It runs its block, then waits until every child it started has finished.

31
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.146
Here the block launches all three calls and awaits nothing, so its last line runs right away.

32
00:01:52.146 --> 00:01:54.192
When does coroutineScope return?

33
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.318
At 300 milliseconds.

34
00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:03.789
The scope can't close while a child is still running, even one that nobody awaited.

35
00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:08.007
So when a suspend function returns, the work it started is done.

36
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:10.910
Failure takes the same path.

37
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:19.459
If fjord-fx throws at 120 milliseconds, the scope cancels the other two, waits for them to stop, and rethrows.

38
00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:21.169
The call fails at 120.

39
00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:26.485
This is Kotlin only: the builders come from kotlinx.coroutines.

40
00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:30.459
Start every async inside coroutineScope, then awaitAll.

41
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.819
So launch and async start a child and return at once.

42
00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:36.595
await waits for one value.

43
00:02:36.595 --> 00:02:40.351
coroutineScope waits for every child before it returns.

44
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:41.448
Quick check.

45
00:02:41.448 --> 00:02:48.992
Inside coroutineScope, you async a 300 millisecond quote, and launch a 500 millisecond audit write.

46
00:02:48.992 --> 00:02:50.897
You await only the quote.

47
00:02:50.897 --> 00:02:52.955
When does the scope return?
